# Why Does This Work?

The input array is already in **level order**. For a complete binary tree, the number of nodes at each level follows:

```text
Level 0 → 1 node
Level 1 → 2 nodes
Level 2 → 4 nodes
Level 3 → 8 nodes
...
```

Therefore, instead of constructing the tree, we can directly split the array into chunks of sizes:

```text
1 → 2 → 4 → 8 → ...
```

Sort each chunk independently and append it to the result.

---

# Time Complexity Analysis

## Brute Force

### Tree Construction

```text
O(n)
```

### BFS Traversal

```text
O(n)
```

### Sorting Each Level

If the levels contain `1, 2, 4, 8, ...` nodes, the total sorting cost is:

[
\sum k_i \log k_i
]

where (k_i) is the number of nodes at level (i). In the worst case, this is bounded by:

```text
O(n log n)
```

### Total Time

```text
O(n log n)
```

### Auxiliary Space

Tree Nodes

```text
O(n)
```

Queue

```text
O(n)
```

Answer

```text
O(n)
```

Overall

```text
O(n)
```

---

## Optimal

### Extract Every Element Once

```text
O(n)
```

### Sorting Each Level

Again,

[
\sum k_i \log k_i \le O(n \log n)
]

Worst case:

```text
O(n log n)
```

### Total Time

```text
O(n log n)
```

### Auxiliary Space

Result

```text
O(n)
```

Temporary level slices across iterations are not held simultaneously; the dominant extra space remains the output structure.

Overall

```text
O(n)
```

